<div class="card">
  <h1>Welcome to the OAuth 2.1 Demo Client</h1>
  <p>
    This Ruby on Rails application demonstrates a secure, production-grade OAuth 2.1 and OpenID Connect (OIDC) client implementation authenticating against Spring Authorization Server.
  </p>

  <% if @authenticated %>
    <div class="alert alert-notice" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
      <strong>You are currently authenticated!</strong> Sub: <code><%= session[:user]["sub"] %></code>
      <div style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
        <a href="/profile" class="btn btn-secondary">View Profile & Token Details &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <div style="margin-top: 1.5rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem;">
      <div>
        <h3>Initiate Authentication Flow</h3>
        <p>Click below to kick off the hardened OAuth 2.1 authorization code flow with backchannel PAR, sender-constrained DPoP, and PKCE:</p>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-group" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
        <%= button_to auth_start_path(flow: "par"), method: :post, class: "btn" do %>
          🚀 Start Secure Login (PAR + DPoP + PKCE)
        <% end %>

        <%= button_to auth_start_path(flow: "direct"), method: :post, class: "btn btn-secondary" do %>
          🌐 Start Direct Authorize (Standard Flow + DPoP + PKCE)
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Security Architecture & Features</h2>
  <p>
    This client adheres strictly to modern OAuth 2.1 and OpenID Connect specifications, enforcing zero-trust sender constraints and cryptographic authentication:
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>RFC 9126: Pushed Authorization Requests (PAR):</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          All authorization parameters (PKCE challenge, state, nonce) are pushed directly via an authenticated backchannel POST to <code>/oauth2/par</code>. Only an opaque, short-lived <code>request_uri</code> is passed through the browser, stopping URL query leakage, browser history snooping, and parameter tampering.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>RFC 7523: Private Key JWT Client Authentication:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          No static client secrets are used. The client authenticates using a 2048-bit RSA key pair (RS256) signing a short-lived client assertion JWT with unique <code>jti</code> replay protection and strict audience binding. Shared secret methods (<code>client_secret_basic</code>, <code>client_secret_post</code>) are strictly rejected.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>RFC 9449: Demonstrating Proof-of-Possession (DPoP) &amp; Server-Provided Nonces:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Tokens are sender-constrained at the application layer. Ephemeral asymmetric keys generate signed DPoP proofs for token requests and resource server calls. The server enforces single-use Redis nonces (RFC 9449 §8) to stop replay attacks within the clock-skew window, and the client transparently handles <code>use_dpop_nonce</code> challenges with automated retries.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>OpenID Connect ID Token Hash Validation (<code>at_hash</code> &amp; <code>c_hash</code>):</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          The client cryptographically validates SHA-256 left-half hashes in the ID Token matching the Access Token (<code>at_hash</code>) and Authorization Code (<code>c_hash</code>), preventing code and token substitution or injection attacks.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>RFC 7636: Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE S256):</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Enforces cryptographic code challenge on all authorization requests, mitigating code interception and injection attacks on public or shared network paths.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>RFC 9207: Authorization Server Issuer Identification:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          The authorization callback strictly validates that the returned <code>iss</code> parameter matches the expected authorization server URL, preventing OAuth 2.0 mix-up attacks.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Server-Determined Authorization Scopes:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          The client sends no scope parameter. The Authorization Server pre-determines and binds authorized scopes strictly based on the registered client identity, mitigating privilege escalation and scope tampering.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>RFC 7009 & RFC 7662: Token Revocation & Introspection:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Allows immediate revocation of access/refresh tokens upon user sign-out, and enables real-time token introspection to detect fraudulent or deactivated sessions before executing high-risk operations.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          The Authorization Server sends signed <code>logout_token</code> JWS requests directly to the client's backchannel endpoint (<code>/oidc/backchannel_logout</code>), instantly evicting active user sessions from Redis.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Redis Session Storage & Rotating Refresh Tokens:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Client sessions persist across reboots in Redis (DB 1). Short-lived access tokens (15m) are transparently refreshed using single-use refresh tokens with automatic token rotation.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>High-Speed Ephemeral DPoP Key Generation (EC P-256 / ES256):</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Proof-of-possession asymmetric keys default to Elliptic Curve P-256 (<code>prime256v1</code> / <code>ES256</code>), generating in <strong>~0.01 ms (4,800x faster than RSA-2048)</strong>. Eliminates ~40ms of CPU blocking time per authentication session under heavy concurrent load.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Thread-Safe In-Memory JWKS Cache with Auto-Rotation:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Authorization Server public keys are cached in-memory with a 1-hour sliding TTL, resolving token verifications in <strong>&lt; 1 ms</strong> without network overhead. Automatically detects and re-fetches unknown <code>kid</code> key rotations with rate-limiting protection.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="check-icon">✓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Automated Network Resilience & Exponential Backoff Retries:</strong>
        <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
          Idempotent operations (JWKS retrieval, token introspection, userinfo, token revocation) are protected with automated exponential backoff retries and randomized jitter to absorb transient connection resets and network glitches.
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Configuration Details</h2>
  <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9rem; text-align: left;">
    <tbody>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted); width: 220px;">Authorization Server</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><code><%= @auth_server_url %></code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted);">Client ID</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><code><%= @client_id %></code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted);">Redirect URI</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><code><%= @redirect_uri %></code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted);">Authentication Method</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><code>private_key_jwt</code> (RFC 7523 - RS256, strictly enforced)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted);">Token Protection</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><code>DPoP</code> (RFC 9449 - Sender-Constrained)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted);">Requested Scopes</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><em>None (Strictly Server-Determined & Pre-Assigned)</em></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--text-muted);">Client Config Storage</td>
        <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;">
          PostgreSQL Database (<code>oauth2_registered_client</code>) &middot;
          <a href="http://localhost:3001" target="_blank" style="color: var(--primary); text-decoration: underline;">Open Next.js Client Manager &rarr;</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
